          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 28-year-old male presents to your primary health centre with a first episode of schizophrenia. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line pharmacological treatment?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Haloperidol (a typical antipsychotic)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Typical (first-generation) antipsychotics such as haloperidol are effective against positive symptoms but carry a higher risk of extrapyramidal side effects. They are not preferred as first-line in first-episode schizophrenia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Clonazepam (a benzodiazepine)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Benzodiazepines may be used short-term for acute agitation or insomnia but do not treat the core psychotic symptoms. An antipsychotic must be initiated.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Risperidone (an atypical antipsychotic)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Second-generation (atypical) antipsychotics such as risperidone, olanzapine, quetiapine, and aripiprazole are the recommended first-line pharmacotherapy owing to a more favourable side-effect profile.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Fluoxetine (an SSRI)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>SSRIs are first-line for depressive and anxiety disorders. Schizophrenia requires antipsychotic therapy that targets dopaminergic pathways, not serotonin reuptake inhibition alone.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is an atypical (second-generation) antipsychotic such as risperidone. SGAs are preferred first-line because they have a lower extrapyramidal side-effect burden compared to typical antipsychotics."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. At primary care, initiating an atypical antipsychotic is the cornerstone of first-episode schizophrenia management, alongside family psychoeducation and timely specialist referral."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
